ran...@pactechdata.com wrote:
> Kent Paul Dolan <xanth...@well.com> wrote:

> YANI: equal rights for the bugs on the floor.

> [ Feedback from engraving via wands which refers
> to "bugs on the floor" could be changed to
> actually summon bug monsters. ]

Not quite "changed to"; more "supplemented by"
actually summoning bug monsters. It wouldn't make
much sense to have the monsters suddenly pop up
without the message displaying first.

[Though, now you make me think of it, having to
determine the wand type by figuring out if the bugs
generated were hasted, slowed, asleep, or dead,
rather than getting the message, is an added
interesting possibility for making engrave testing
less mind numbingly easy.]

> That would turn every wand which references bugs
> into a ``wand of create sacrifice fodder''.

Yep. That was intended (and mentioned in the OP).

By making this a new "resource management" issue of
having to choose between the normal use of wand
charges, and using them in this new way, the game
play would be a bit enriched.

Would it be overpowered? Maybe. Not that many
players seem to attempt sac fests now, so I don't
know that it would be, in general. It might need
tweaking by changing the wand generation
probabilities to make less of these type wands,
though.

One moderating possibility would be to disqualify
former "bugs on the floor" from having death drops,
which is an idea already suggested in several other
contexts, including pudding farming.

YAN(?)I: So perhaps a useful flag to add to the
monsters' flags field would be "randomly generated"
and only originally randomly generated monsters
could have death drops, while monsters farmed,
zapped into existence, reverse genocided, summoning
stormed, or whatever, could not.

That way such monsters would still be useful purely
as sac fodder, but would not also be "monty haul
game style" super-abundant death drop goodie
generating mechanisms.

Quantum valeat.

